      <h1 class="page-title">AI-Native Enterprise Transformation</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70">The global economy is entering a structural transformation as profound as the Industrial Revolution — but unfolding in a fraction of the time. AI-native enterprises create the conditions for continuous growth, efficiency, and resilience by putting AI at the core of their business.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section reveal">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 lg:px-6">
      <div class="prose-content">
        <p>Becoming an AI-native enterprise is not about deploying a few machine learning models or piloting a chatbot. It is a fundamental reimagining of how the organisation operates — where AI capabilities are woven into core processes, decision-making, and customer interactions. Organisations that treat AI as a side project will find themselves outpaced by competitors who have made it foundational.</p>

        <h2>What This Means for You</h2>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>AI is no longer a pilot project; it is a core business capability that shapes operating models and competitive strategy</li>
          <li>Organisations must reimagine processes, not just automate them — incremental automation of legacy workflows yields incremental results</li>
          <li>Workforce readiness is becoming a critical differentiator — the organisations that invest in skills, change management, and culture will adapt faster</li>
          <li>Continuous learning and adaptation are essential for sustained advantage — AI capabilities evolve rapidly, and so must the organisation</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Our Approach</h2>
        <p>We help you build an AI-native advantage through a structured, outcome-driven methodology:</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Assessing your AI readiness across technology, data, processes, and people — identifying gaps and prioritising investments</li>
          <li>Developing an AI transformation roadmap aligned to business outcomes — not technology for its own sake</li>
          <li>Implementing AI platforms and workflows that scale — from pilot to production with appropriate governance</li>
          <li>Building workforce capabilities for the AI era — skills development, change management, and culture transformation</li>
          <li>Establishing governance frameworks for responsible AI adoption — guardrails that manage risk without stifling innovation</li>
        </ul>

        <blockquote>AI-native transformation is not measured by the number of models deployed, but by the business outcomes those models enable — efficiency, agility, and new value creation that would not have been possible otherwise.</blockquote>

        <h2>The MEA Context</h2>
        <p>For organisations across the Middle East and Africa, AI-native transformation presents both opportunity and complexity. National AI strategies in the Gulf, growing digital talent pools across Africa, and region-specific data sovereignty requirements shape how AI capabilities can be deployed. The organisations that succeed will be those that align AI investments to their specific business context — not those that copy a generic playbook.</p>
      </div>
